{"version":"6.9","hitCount":1,"request":{"queryString":"TITLE:\"Naturally occurring antibodies devoid of light chains\" AND SRC:MED","resultType":"core","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":6,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"8502296","source":"MED","pmid":"8502296","doi":"10.1038/363446a0","title":"Naturally occurring antibodies devoid of light chains.","authorString":"Hamers-Casterman C, Atarhouch T, Muyldermans S, Robinson G, Hamers C, Songa EB, Bendahman N, Hamers R.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Hamers-Casterman C","lastName":"Hamers-Casterman","firstName":"C","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Vrije Universiteit Brussel, Instituut voor Moleculaire Biologie, Belgium."}]}},{"fullName":"Atarhouch T","lastName":"Atarhouch","firstName":"T","initials":"T"},{"fullName":"Muyldermans S","lastName":"Muyldermans","firstName":"S","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3678-3575"}},{"fullName":"Robinson G","lastName":"Robinson","firstName":"G","initials":"G"},{"fullName":"Hamers C","lastName":"Hamers","firstName":"C","initials":"C"},{"fullName":"Songa EB","lastName":"Songa","firstName":"E B","initials":"EB"},{"fullName":"Bendahman N","lastName":"Bendahman","firstName":"N","initials":"N"},{"fullName":"Hamers R","lastName":"Hamers","firstName":"R","initials":"R"}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3678-3575"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["wikipedia"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"6428","volume":"363","journalIssueId":682190,"dateOfPublication":"1993 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":1993,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Nature","title":"Nature","essn":"1476-4687","issn":"0028-0836","nlmid":"0410462","isoabbreviation":"Nature"},"printPublicationDate":"1993-06-01"},"pubYear":"1993","pageInfo":"446-448","abstractText":"Random association of VL and VH repertoires contributes considerably to antibody diversity. The diversity and the affinity are then increased by hypermutation in B cells located in germinal centres. Except in the case of 'heavy chain' disease, naturally occurring heavy-chain antibodies have not been described, although antigen binding has been demonstrated for separated heavy chains or cloned VH domains. Here we investigate the presence of considerable amounts of IgG-like material of M(r) 100K in the serum of the camel (Camelus dromedarius). These molecules are composed of heavy-chain dimers and are devoid of light chains, but nevertheless have an extensive antigen-binding repertoire, a finding that calls into question the role of light chains in the camel. Camel heavy-chain IgGs lack CH1, which in one IgG class might be structurally replaced by an extended hinge. Heavy-chain IgGs are a feature of all camelids. These findings open new perspectives in the engineering of antibodies.","affiliation":"Vrije Universiteit Brussel, Instituut voor Moleculaire Biologie, Belgium.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Trypanosomiasis","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"IM","qualifierName":"immunology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"VE","qualifierName":"veterinary"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Immunoglobulin G","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CL","qualifierName":"classification"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Immunoglobulin Variable Region","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"DNA, Single-Stranded"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Antigens, Protozoan","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[